{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-183567",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that our sentence can be re-phrased as \n\nThere exists a rational number $q$ whose natural powers are all less than or equal to $q.$\n\nNow, \"the natural powers of $q$ are all less than or equal to $q$\" means that\n\nFor any natural number $n,$ we have that ${q}^{n}\\le q.$\n\nTherefore, we get the following statement:\n\nThere exists a rational number $q$ such that for any natural number $n$ we have that ${q}^{n}\\le q.$\n\nMore formally, this reads as\n\n$∃\\,q\\in Q,\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,{q}^{n}\\le q.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
